            <ArticleTitle>Fear-avoidance, pain acceptance and adjustment to chronic pain: a cross-sectional study on a sample of 686 patients with chronic spinal pain.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>402-10</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1007/s12160-014-9619-6</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Prior studies found a range of psychological factors related to the perception of pain, maintenance of pain and disability.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PURPOSE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">The aim of this study was to investigate the role of pain fear-avoidance and pain acceptance in chronic pain adjustment. The influence of two diathesis variables (resilience and experiential avoidance) was also analyzed.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">The sample was composed of 686 patients with chronic spinal pain. Structural equation modelling analyses were used to test the hypothetical model.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Experiential avoidance was associated with pain fear-avoidance, and resilience was strongly associated with pain acceptance. Pain acceptance was negatively associated with negative mood, functional impairment and pain intensity. However, pain fear-avoidance was positively and significantly associated with negative mood but had no association with pain intensity. There was a path from functional impairment to pain fear-avoidance.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Resilience and experiential avoidance appear as variables which could explain individual differences in pain experience.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
